Rome began as a small city-state in Italy, expanding through might and alliances.
At its height, under Emperor Trajan, it stretched from Britain to Egypt and Mesopotamia.

The Roman legacy lies in engineering, architecture, and law.
Its aqueducts supplied clean water, public baths were massive, and structures like the Colosseum stood strong.

Roman Law established concepts of citizenship and property rights.
