2025-10-29 08:49:37,599 - INFO - RESPONSE: The Voyager Program consists of two robotic probes, Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, launched by NASA in 1977 to take advantage of a rare planetary alignment that occurs only once every 175 years.

This alignment allowed the spacecraft to use gravity assists from Jupiter and Saturn to slingshot to the ice giants, Uranus and Neptune, dramatically reducing the travel time and fuel required for the ambitious mission, which became known as the Grand Tour.

The primary mission for both spacecraft was the exploration of Jupiter and Saturn, their major moons, and the structure of their magnetospheres and rings.

Upon reaching Jupiter in 1979, the Voyagers provided stunning, high-resolution images and discovered three new moons: Metis, Adrastea, and Thebe.

Most notably, they observed active volcanoes on Io, making it the first known volcanically active body other than Earth.

They also provided detailed data on Jupiter’s Great Red Spot and the complex dynamics of its atmosphere.

At Saturn in 1980 and 1981, the probes returned incredible images of the ring system, revealing thousands of intricate ringlets and strange features like the "spokes" and "braided" rings.

The flyby of Saturn’s moon Titan provided confirmation of a dense, nitrogen-rich atmosphere, inspiring future dedicated missions.

Following the Saturn encounter, Voyager 1 used a final gravity assist to propel it out of the plane of the planets, setting it on a course to become the first probe to enter interstellar space.

Voyager 2 was uniquely tasked with continuing its journey to the distant ice giants.

It reached Uranus in 1986, discovering 10 new moons, including Cordelia and Ophelia, and a new set of rings.

The spacecraft observed Uranus’s bizarre 98-degree axial tilt and its highly irregular magnetic field.

Three years later, in 1989, it encountered Neptune, the last planetary target.

Here, it discovered the Great Dark Spot, a storm system similar to Jupiter's, and six new moons.

The flyby of Triton, Neptune’s largest moon, revealed a cold, icy world with active geysers, a major surprise that suggested geological activity far from the Sun.

Voyager 1 officially entered interstellar space in 2012.
